  

<h2 id="also-image-reduction-online-graffiti-and-a-blot.im-update">Also, image reduction, online graffiti, and a Blot.im update</h2>
<p><img src="https://cdn.blot.im/blog_177bc256f0ee469c998e912e20ece048/_image_cache/14bfeefb-2f68-45b2-91ac-dd371651658f/pasted-percent20image-percent2020261026110504.png" title="Photo of a small notebook with the front cover opened to show a hand drawn 15 cm ruler on the back" alt="Photo of a small notebook with the front cover opened to show a hand drawn 15 cm ruler on the back" width="1622" height="1252"></p>
<h2 id="productivity-inspiration">Productivity inspiration</h2>
<h3 id="paper-notebook-mods">Paper notebook mods</h3>
<p>I drew a ruler in the front of my <span class="small-caps">A6</span> notebook after seeing <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=tOMtfJNYozI&amp;t=2s" target="_blank">this video</a> from Lilla Björn Stationery. It’s quick to draw up months the way Lilla shows, which is also Ryder Carroll’s method, but I strongly prefer standard calendar-style boxes on the page. Watch this space for piccies of next year’s notebook once it’s drawn up.</p>
<p><img src="https://cdn.blot.im/blog_177bc256f0ee469c998e912e20ece048/_image_cache/b4054cd2-ba29-4fed-9f95-4c1a387a80c4/pasted-percent20image-percent2020261026145655.png" title="Photo of the inside back cover of a small notebook, showing a handmade card pocket attached to it. There are plants and leaves drawn roughly in pencil on the card pocket." alt="Photo of the inside back cover of a small notebook, showing a handmade card pocket attached to it. There are plants and leaves drawn roughly in pencil on the card pocket." width="2230" height="1676"></p>
<p>Lilla also made a pocket for the back of her little notebook. If you decide to add an elastic closure band, I recommend modifying Lilla’s instructions. Don’t cut the band once you’ve poked in through the back cover; thread a paper clip through it instead. This will lie a lot flatter than the knot she shows in the video.</p>
<h3 id="image-reduction-and-online-graffiti">Image reduction, and online graffiti</h3>
<p>Here’s a no-cost online <a href="https://utilvance.com/tools/reduce-image-size.php" target="_blank">image size reducer</a> from Utilvance. It handles <span class="small-caps">JPG</span>, <span class="small-caps">PNG</span>, and WebP. Privacy looks good, and you can specify the target file size.</p>
<p>Brett Terpstra’s public <a href="https://graffiti.moe/add" target="_blank">graffiti wall</a> is a fun way to leave your mark. It’s terminal-friendly, but you don’t need to know anything about the command line interface to write on the wall.</p>
<h2 id="adventures-in-plain-text">Adventures in plain text</h2>
<h3 id="markdown-calendar">Markdown calendar</h3>
<p>I’m loving my plain text outline calendar. Like, really loving it! It gets out the way and is a nice blend of simplicity and functionality. Past experience tells me this kind of enthusiasm can’t be expected to last more than 3 months, but I suspect the simplicity of a simple outline (shown in <a href="https://ellanew.com/2026/10/19/ptpl-229-pencil-is-permanent-appending-obtf" class="wikilink">last week’s post</a> will be my friend for a long time yet.</p>
<p>This week I removed all the blank lines from my calendar file to simplify the quick, keyboard shortcut-activated moving of events up and down the file. While I love the look of neatly placed blank lines in a Markdown document, I don’t love having to re-sort them into place when things inevitably get moved around.</p>
<p>I’ve learned a lot about fully manual, plain text calendars since the first experiment over a year ago. Plans are in the works to write up all this geekish wisdom into a more easily digestible form.</p>
<h3 id="blot.im-now-supports-embedded-text">Blot.im now supports embedded text</h3>
<p>For those of you who don’t know, my personal website is simply a collection of text files inside an Obsidian vault. Everything in the <em>Posts</em> folder is automatically published. While I would one day like to learn how to code my own static site, for now I’m very happy with Blot.im.</p>
<p>Good news: Blot now recognises embedded text! <code>![[document.md]]</code> and <code>![[image.jpg]]</code> render online the same way they do in Obsidian preview mode.</p>
